.TH CDM 1 "2026-08-15" "ctrl-data-mgmt 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cdm \- local file metadata catalog
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cdm
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ path ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cdm
indexes file metadata \(em size, timestamps, inode, and optionally a content
hash \(em into a local SQLite database, then answers questions about it without
touching the filesystem again.
.PP
Roots are explicit. There is no default
.B $HOME
crawl and no daemon. Nothing leaves the machine, and the tool has no runtime
dependencies beyond the Python standard library.
.PP
Queries are answered from the index, so
.B cdm du
over a hundred thousand files returns in a fraction of a second where
.BR du (1)
must re-walk the tree every time. The trade is that the index is only as fresh
as the last
.BR "cdm rescan" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "scan " path ...
Add each
.I path
as a root and index everything beneath it. Re-running on a known root behaves
as
.BR rescan .
.TP
.BI "rescan " [ path ...]
Re-index the named roots, or every known root if none are named. Files whose
size and mtime are unchanged keep their existing hash, so a quiet tree costs
one
.BR stat (2)
per file.
.TP
.B roots
List indexed roots with their entry counts and last scan time. Each entry is
counted once, under its most specific root; a root nested in another is marked
.RI "(inside " outer ).
See
.BR "NESTED ROOTS" .
.TP
.BI "forget " path
Remove a root and the rows it owns from the index. Rows owned by a root nested
inside it are kept. If another root encloses it, its rows are handed to that
root instead of deleted, since that root's scans cover them anyway. Nothing on
disk is read, written, or deleted.
.TP
.BI "find " [ options ]
Query the index. All filters are optional and combine with AND.
.TP
.BI "du " [ path ]
Sum file sizes by subdirectory, answered from the index. Directory inodes are
excluded from the totals, since a directory's own size is not the space its
contents occupy.
.TP
.BI "dupes " [ options ]
Report files that share a hash of the same kind and an identical size.
Requires a previous scan with
.B \-\-checksum
or
.BR \-\-full\-checksum .
With
.B \-\-verify
any file that cannot be read is reported and excluded from the totals, and the
command exits non-zero: a verification that could not read a file has not
verified anything about it. Its own reads are remembered so they do not count
as use (see
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" );
if a file's access time cannot be read back afterwards, that is reported too.
.TP
.BI "suggest " [ options ]
List what is worth doing, ranked, with the command for each. Changes nothing.
See
.BR SUGGESTIONS .
.TP
.BI "policy " options
Print a script that lists an IBM Storage Scale fileset with the policy engine,
for an administrator to run. See
.BR "STORAGE SCALE" .
.TP
.BI "guide " [ options ]
Show the getting\-started checklist and what to do next. See
.BR GUIDE .
.TP
.BI "stat " path
Print everything the index records about one path, including whether its hash
has gone stale and, where they are known, when the file was last read and
whether it has been opened since it last changed. See
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" .
.TP
.B mcp
Serve the index to an AI client over the Model Context Protocol, on standard
input and output. Read\-only. See
.BR "MCP SERVER" .
.TP
.B doctor
Report index location, permissions, entry and hash counts, stale hashes, how
many files have a trusted access time and, for each root, why or why not (see
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" ),
and roots that have disappeared from disk. Exits non-zero if the index, its
write-ahead log, its shared-memory file or the data directory are readable by
other users, so it can gate a script rather than merely mentioning the problem.
.SH SCAN OPTIONS
Accepted by
.B scan
and
.BR rescan .
.TP
.BR \-j " " \-\-threads " N"
Number of walker threads. The default is measured, not guessed: a sample of
stat calls is timed at startup and the tool picks 1 thread for a local
filesystem and up to 8 for a remote one. See
.BR CONCURRENCY .
.TP
.B \-\-restart
Ignore any checkpoint from an interrupted scan and start from the beginning.
.TP
.B \-\-checksum
Record a partial hash: the first and last 64 KiB plus the exact byte count.
A file of 128 KiB or less is read whole.
Intended for duplicate detection.
.TP
.B \-\-full\-checksum
Record a hash of every byte. Intended for integrity checking.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-hash\-size " size"
Do not hash files larger than
.IR size .
They are still indexed, just without a hash.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " glob"
Skip paths matching
.IR glob .
May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-skip\-credentials
Index credential paths, which are excluded by default. See
.BR EXCLUSIONS .
.TP
.BR \-\-progress " [" \fIsecs\fR ]
Write a timestamped line to standard error every
.I secs
seconds (default 10) giving entries scanned, entries per second, bytes hashed,
hash throughput and elapsed time. Without this option the same figures are
shown as a single updating line, and only when standard error is a terminal.
.SH FIND OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " glob"
Match the filename. Uses SQL
.B GLOB
semantics: shell wildcards, case sensitive.
.TP
.BI \-\-iname " glob"
Like
.B \-\-name
but case\-insensitive. This is usually what you want on macOS and Windows, whose
filesystems treat
.I Report.TXT
and
.I report.txt
as the same name while SQL
.B GLOB
does not.
.TP
.BI \-\-type " {file|dir|link}"
Restrict to one entry type.
.TP
.BI \-\-larger\-than " size" ", " \-\-smaller\-than " size"
Filter on size. See
.BR "SIZES AND TIMES" .
.TP
.BI \-\-modified\-after " when" ", " \-\-modified\-before " when"
Filter on mtime.
.TP
.BI \-\-accessed\-after " when" ", " \-\-accessed\-before " when"
Filter on last access time. Files without a trusted access time never match
either. See
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" .
.TP
.B \-\-unopened
Only files known not to have been opened since they last changed. See
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" .
.TP
.BI \-\-root " path"
Restrict results to one root, including any roots nested inside it.
.TP
.BI \-\-order " {size|mtime|atime|name|path}"
Sort order. Default
.BR size ,
descending.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " n"
Maximum rows. Default 100.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-hosts
Include rows recorded by other hosts. By default only the local host's rows
are considered.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print bare paths only, one per line, for piping.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print rows as JSON.
.SH SIZES AND TIMES
Sizes accept an optional binary unit:
.BR 4096 ,
.BR 1k ,
.BR 100M ,
.BR 2.5G ,
.BR 1T .
Units are powers of 1024.
.PP
Times accept a relative span \(em
.BR 30s ,
.BR 10m ,
.BR 24h ,
.BR 7d ,
.B 2w
\(em or an absolute date as
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD .
.SH HASHING
Two hash kinds exist because there are two different questions, and each row
records which kind it holds so the two can never be confused.
.TP
.B partial
Answers \fIare these probably the same file\fR. Reads 64 KiB from each end and
mixes in the byte count. On a large tree this is the difference between minutes
and hours.
.TP
.B full
Answers \fIis this byte\-for\-byte what I recorded\fR. Reads everything. No
shortcut exists.
.PP
.B "cdm dupes \-\-verify"
re\-reads partial\-hash candidates in full and reports only groups that survive:
the partial hash proposes, the full hash confirms.
.PP
Each hash also records the size and mtime it was computed against. A file
modified after it was hashed is reported as
.B STALE
rather than silently returning a hash that is no longer true.
.SH CONCURRENCY
Walking a filesystem is latency\-bound, not CPU\-bound, and CPython releases the
interpreter lock around
.BR scandir (3)
and
.BR stat (2),
so threads help here despite the usual advice about the GIL.
.PP
How much they help depends entirely on how far away the filesystem is.
Measured against simulated metadata latency:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l.
per\-operation latency	8 threads	32 threads
0.1ms (fast)	7.1x	14.9x
0.5ms (typical remote)	7.5x	26.9x
2ms (loaded server)	7.9x	30.4x
.TE
.RE
.PP
On a warm local disk the same threading is a
.B 4x slowdown
\(em there is no latency to hide, so all concurrency adds is contention. That
is why the default is measured rather than fixed, and why it is 1 locally.
.PP
The remote default is deliberately conservative. On a shared cluster you are
one of many users of the metadata servers, and enough concurrent traffic to be
noticed by everyone else is a worse outcome than a slower scan. Raise it with
.B \-j
when you know the system can take it.
.PP
Hashing is always single\-threaded. It is bandwidth\-bound rather than
latency\-bound, so concurrency buys little and saturating shared storage costs a
lot.
.SH RESUMING
An interrupted scan can be resumed. Each directory is checkpointed in the same
database transaction as the entries it contains, so a scan killed at any point
leaves the index consistent: every checkpointed directory has its rows, and
nothing is marked done that was not written.
.PP
Re\-running
.B "cdm scan"
on the same root picks up where it left off and skips the directories already
recorded. A resumed scan is faster than a fresh one, not slower. Use
.B \-\-restart
to discard the checkpoint.
.PP
A checkpoint is only resumed when the hash kind matches. Resuming a stat\-only
scan with
.B \-\-checksum
would leave part of the tree hashed and part not, with nothing recording which
part.
.PP
Checkpoints are deleted when a scan completes.
.PP
A root may contain the index itself, as a scan of the home directory does.
Those files are recorded but never opened for hashing: closing any descriptor
to a file drops every POSIX lock the process holds on it, and losing SQLite's
locks lets another process delete the write\-ahead log while the scan is still
using it.
.SH ACCESS TIMES
For files,
.B cdm
records the last access time, but only where a read of a file last read more
than a day ago moves it: Linux
.B relatime
semantics or better. A filesystem mounted
.B noatime
or read\-only fails that, and so does macOS APFS, where a read was measured to
move the access time only on the first read after a change. Elsewhere the
access time is recorded as unknown, never as a date.
.PP
Trust is decided per device. On the device holding the index directory it is
measured with a scratch file; on others it is read from the mount options, and
on macOS an unmeasured device is not trusted.
.PP
The scanner's own reads are not counted as use. On Linux, hashing opens files
with
.BR O_NOATIME .
Otherwise the access time left by
.BR cdm 's
read is remembered, and the earlier access time stands until something else
reads the file.
.B "dupes \-\-verify"
does the same. Directories are never given an access time, since scanning
lists them.
.B "cdm doctor"
reports how many files have a trusted access time.
If the measurement itself fails, a scan says so and falls back to mount
options, and
.B "cdm doctor"
gives the reason.
.PP
A narrower fact survives where last read does not: whether a file has been
opened at all since it last changed. Wherever the first read after a change
moves the access time \(em every filesystem above except
.BR noatime ,
read\-only ones and unmeasured macOS devices \(em
.B cdm
records that a file was not opened between its last change and a date, and
always shows the date. On macOS its own first read uses the evidence up, so
the date stops at that read; with
.B O_NOATIME
on Linux it advances with each scan. For an index built before this was
recorded, on macOS the answer is recovered from when earlier hashing scans read
each file.
.PP
To check a filesystem the probe cannot reach, such as a network or parallel
filesystem, point
.B CDM_DATA_DIR
at a directory on it, scan, and run
.BR "cdm doctor" .
.SH NESTED ROOTS
Roots may overlap, for example
.I ~
and
.I ~/src
both registered. Each path has a single row in the index, owned by the most
specific root containing it no matter which scan wrote it last. A scan of
either root reuses hashes the other computed, and prunes vanished files in
every directory it read, including those owned by a nested root.
.PP
.B "find \-\-root"
and the MCP tools scoped to a root include the roots nested inside it.
.B du
and
.B dupes
work by path, so overlapping roots are never counted twice.
.PP
The cost is that
.B "cdm rescan"
walks the overlap once per root.
.B "cdm scan"
notes when a new root overlaps an existing one.
.SH SUGGESTIONS
.B "cdm suggest"
reads the index and lists things worth doing: package and application caches,
dependencies and build output untouched for a while, large git histories, old
installers and downloaded archives, AI model files, duplicates, and index
housekeeping. Each comes with its size, the reason, a risk and the command a
person would run.
.B cdm
never runs these commands.
.PP
Risk is
.B none
for housekeeping (a stale scan, an unhashed root, stale hashes), listed first
because it decides whether every other answer is right;
.B safe
for data that regenerates on its own, such as a package cache; and
.B review
for everything else. Age is the later of the last modification and, where it
is trusted, the last read (see
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" ).
A recent read may be an indexer or a backup rather than a person, so nothing
judged by age alone is marked
.BR safe .
Dependencies and build output are only suggested inside a git working tree.
A cache inside another listed cache is not counted twice, but savings can
overlap between suggestions.
.TP
.BI \-\-older\-than " days"
How long dependencies and build output must be unmodified, as
.I N
or
.IR N d.
Default 90.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " path"
Only this root and roots nested in it.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " n"
Show at most
.I n
suggestions (default 20).
.TP
.BI \-\-items " n"
Show at most
.I n
paths per suggestion (default 5);
.B \-\-all
shows every one.
.TP
.B \-\-json
The same data the MCP
.B suggest
tool returns.
.SH GUIDE
.B "cdm guide"
prints a fixed checklist from first scan to a well\-kept index, with the next
step explained: index a folder; hash it; see what's worth doing; clean up, then
rescan; keep it current; and, optionally, connect an AI client. Each step shows
the command to run and what you will see afterwards.
.PP
A step is marked done only because the index shows it: a root exists, at least
90% of its files are hashed, every root was scanned in the last 7 days. Steps
the index cannot observe, such as whether you acted on a suggestion, are shown
as advice and never as done. Progress counts the three steps that can always
be checked.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Explain every step, not just the next one.
.TP
.B \-\-schedule
Print a job that runs
.B "cdm rescan \-\-checksum"
nightly: a launchd agent on macOS, a crontab line elsewhere. The job goes to
standard output, so it can be redirected into place; how to install and remove
it goes to standard error.
.B cdm
never installs it. The job runs the
.B cdm
found on
.BR PATH ,
or, if there is none, the current Python with
.BR "\-m cdm" ;
the path is escaped for the plist and quoted for the shell cron uses.
.RS
.PP
.EX
cdm guide \-\-schedule > \e
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.ctrl\-data\-mgmt.rescan.plist
launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id \-u) \e
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.ctrl\-data\-mgmt.rescan.plist
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-json
The same checklist the MCP
.B guide
tool returns.
.SH STORAGE SCALE
On IBM Storage Scale a directory walk is the slow path; the policy engine reads
inode metadata directly.
.B "cdm policy"
prints a POSIX shell script for an administrator to review and run as root on a
node that mounts the filesystem.
.B cdm
never runs it and never needs root. The script runs one
.B LIST
rule with
.BR "mmapplypolicy \-I defer" ,
which reads metadata and changes no files, and writes the listing with a header
(filesystem, cluster, scope, the filesystem's
.B \-S
access\-time setting, field order) and an end marker with the row count, so a
truncated listing can be told from a complete one. The listing is written mode
0600 through a temporary file, so an interrupted run leaves nothing that looks
whole.
.PP
The listing names every file it covers. Its name must end in
.BR .raw ,
which keeps it out of the repository (see
.IR tests/fixtures/README.md );
keep it private and delete it once imported. Reading it into the index,
.BR "cdm import \-\-policy" ,
is planned for 0.2.0 and not in this version.
.TP
.BI \-\-device " fs"
The Storage Scale filesystem device name. Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-fileset " name"
List one fileset. One of this or
.B \-\-whole\-filesystem
is required.
.TP
.B \-\-whole\-filesystem
List every fileset. The index will then hold every user's file names, and the
command says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " file" .raw
Where the script writes the listing. Default
.IB device \- fileset .list.raw
or
.IB device .list.raw ;
the script also accepts it as its first argument.
.TP
.BI \-\-nodes " nodes"
Passed to
.B mmapplypolicy \-N
to share the scan across nodes or node classes, such as
.BR nsdNodes .
.PP
Device, fileset and node names must be plain Storage Scale names \(em letters,
digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.B \-
\(em because they are written into a script run as root and into policy SQL;
anything else is refused rather than escaped.
.SH MCP SERVER
.B "cdm mcp"
lets an MCP client such as Claude Code or Claude Desktop answer plain\-language
questions about the index. It needs Python 3.10 or newer and the optional SDK:
.PP
.RS
.EX
pipx inject ctrl\-data\-mgmt mcp
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without either,
.B "cdm mcp"
says so and exits 2. The rest of
.B cdm
is unaffected and still runs on Python 3.9 with no dependencies.
.PP
.B Names are not exposed by default.
Out of the box the server offers only
.BR summary ,
.BR size_histogram ,
.BR age_histogram ,
.BR extensions ,
.BR duplicates_summary ,
.B suggest
and
.BR guide :
totals, histograms, file types and ranked suggestions, with no file or
directory name below a root. With a cloud\-hosted model every answer goes to
its provider, and filenames are often sensitive on their own. Extensions are
shown only when they look like a file type \(em short, alphanumeric, and shared
by at least two files \(em so a suffix cannot easily carry part of a name.
.TP
.B \-\-expose\-names
Also offer
.BR find ,
.BR du ,
.B dupes
and
.BR stat ,
which return paths, and let
.B suggest
list the paths behind each suggestion. Without this flag those tools are not
registered at all, so a client cannot call them or learn that they exist.
.PP
The server opens the index with SQLite in read\-only mode, so a write is refused
by SQLite itself. It never walks directories and never reads file contents,
which is why there is no scan tool and no equivalent of
.BR "dupes \-\-verify" .
Keep the index current with
.B "cdm rescan"
yourself; the
.B summary
tool reports how many days ago each root was scanned.
.PP
The server also offers prompts, ready\-made questions a client can list as a
menu:
.BR getting\-started ,
.BR disk\-usage ,
.BR cleanup ,
.BR duplicates ,
.B recent\-changes
and
.BR index\-health ,
each taking an optional
.IR root .
Every tool result includes
.BR next_steps ,
naming the next useful tool. The model is told to show the commands
.B suggest
returns and never to run them.
.PP
On start it prints to standard error what it is serving, which tools are live
and whether names are exposed. Standard output carries only protocol messages.
.PP
To use it from Claude Code:
.PP
.RS
.EX
claude mcp add \-\-scope user cdm \-\- cdm mcp
.EE
.RE
.SH UNREADABLE PATHS
A scan concludes that a file is gone by not having seen it. That inference is
only drawn for directories it could actually read.
.PP
If a directory cannot be opened \(em permissions, an unmounted filesystem, or a
path that needs Full Disk Access on macOS \(em then nothing beneath it is
removed from the index, and its previously recorded entries are kept untouched.
Absence is observed, never assumed.
.PP
If the root itself cannot be opened, the scan indexes nothing, removes nothing,
leaves the root's last\-scan time alone, and exits non\-zero. It is reported as
a failure rather than as an empty directory, because the two look identical
from the outside and only one of them is true.
.PP
Unreadable paths are always counted and reported.
.SH EXCLUSIONS
By default
.B cdm
skips credential paths, among them
.BR .ssh ,
.BR .gnupg ,
.BR .aws ,
.BR .kube ,
keychains, browser profiles, and files matching
.B *.pem
or
.BR *.key .
.PP
Symlinks are recorded as entries of type
.B link
but are never followed; a link into
.B /proc
would otherwise turn a scan into an unbounded walk.
.PP
.B .gitignore
is deliberately not honoured. It describes what git should ignore, not what
exists on disk, and disk usage is one of the questions this tool answers.
.PP
Every skipped path is counted and reported. An index that silently omits
entries is worse than one that refuses out loud.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/ctrl-data-mgmt/index.db
The index. Created mode 0600 inside a 0700 directory, because a list of every
filename you own is more revealing than most file contents. Defaults to
.I ~/.local/share/ctrl-data-mgmt/index.db
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.IR index.db\-wal ", " index.db\-shm
SQLite's write\-ahead log and shared\-memory file. They hold the same filename
data as the index and are created by SQLite itself, so they are checked and
corrected to 0600 as well.
.PP
If a mode cannot be set \(em some filesystems do not support
.BR chmod (2)
\(em the tool prints a warning naming the file rather than continuing silently.
The scan still runs; whether that is acceptable is a decision only you can make,
which is why it is reported instead of assumed either way.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/ctrl\-data\-mgmt/.cdm\-atime\-probe\-*
A few\-byte scratch file, created and deleted during each scan and by
.BR "cdm doctor" ,
to measure whether that filesystem keeps last\-read times. It is the only file
.B cdm
writes outside the index, and only ever in its own data directory. See
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CDM_DATA_DIR
Override the data directory.
.TP
.B CDM_INDEX
Override the full path to the index file.
.TP
.B CDM_HOST
Override the hostname recorded in the
.B host
column. Useful when a shared filesystem should be attributed to one logical
name rather than to whichever node performed the scan.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing found, or an unhealthy index \(em an unknown path to
.BR stat ,
an unscanned path to
.BR du ,
a missing index, a vanished root, or permissions that let other users read the
index to
.BR doctor ,
files that could not be read to
.BR "dupes \-\-verify" ,
a root that could not be opened to
.B scan
or
.BR rescan ,
or a missing or out\-of\-date index to
.BR mcp .
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unparseable size or time, or a path that is not a directory.
Also
.B mcp
when the MCP SDK is not installed or Python is older than 3.10.
.TP
.B 3
.B rescan
was asked to run with no roots recorded.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. Rows already committed are kept; the interrupted root keeps its
previous last\-scan time.
.SH EXAMPLES
Index two trees and record dedupe hashes:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cdm scan ~/work ~/datasets \-\-checksum
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find large files nobody has touched this quarter:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cdm find \-\-larger\-than 500M \-\-modified\-before 90d
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what is consuming a tree, two levels down:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cdm du /scratch/project \-\-depth 2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Confirm duplicates before acting on them:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cdm dupes \-\-min\-size 100M \-\-verify
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed results to another tool:
.PP
.RS
.EX
cdm find \-\-name '*.csv' \-\-quiet | xargs wc \-l
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The scanner walks POSIX directories with
.BR scandir (3).
On a parallel filesystem such as IBM Spectrum Scale or Lustre this is the slow
path by design; the native policy engine reads metadata far faster. This
version does not read policy\-engine output; that is planned for 0.2.0.
.PP
Hashing is single\-threaded; walking uses one thread on a local disk and up to
eight on a remote filesystem (see
.BR CONCURRENCY ).
A stat\-only pass runs at roughly 30,000 entries per second on a laptop.
.PP
The
.B host
column is recorded but only ever populated with the local host. Remote scanning
is not implemented.
.PP
On macOS, parts of a home directory \(em
.IR ~/Library/Mail ,
.IR ~/Library/Messages ,
Safari data, the Photos library \(em are protected by the system's privacy
controls and cannot be read without granting Full Disk Access to the terminal
running
.BR cdm .
Without it those paths are reported as unreadable and skipped, which is
correct but means the index is incomplete for them.
.PP
Also on macOS, APFS updates a file's access time only on the first read after
it changes, so last\-read times there are recorded as unknown and only
\(lqnot opened since it changed\(rq is kept. See
.BR "ACCESS TIMES" .
On IBM Storage Scale, access\-time updates are set per filesystem with
.B "mmchfs \-S"
and may not appear in the mount options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR du (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR stat (1),
.BR sqlite3 (1),
.BR pdsh (1)
